@*
    The one word a metadata block leads with: is this current?

    It lives on its own because the answer is wanted next to the heading, not
    three rows down a description list. Before this existed the only way to draw
    a folder-aware status was to hand KnowledgeMetaView a whole block and take
    the <dl> that came with it, so a row of statuses read "status draft status
    proposed status active" — the label repeated before every pill because every
    pill brought its own list with it.

    Nothing here judges the word without knowing where it came from. The same
    status means different things in different folders, so a tone or a warning
    guessed without the folder would be wrong as often as right.

    What it wears is the application's status badge, and it is composed from
    Badge rather than assembled here so there is one implementation of what a
    badge's classes are. KnowledgeStatusBadge says which modifier the folder's
    word maps onto, and the record's headline dresses its StatusSelector out of
    the same call: this is the read-only form of the status, not a second opinion
    about what the status looks like.

    The name is now the one thing about this component that is wrong — it renders
    a badge, not a pill — but renaming it ripples through the storybook coverage
    rule and several test files, so it is left for its own change.
*@

@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Status))
{
    <Badge Kind="status"
           Slug="@KnowledgeStatusBadge.Slug(Folder, Status)"
           Title="@StatusTitle"
           CssClass="@ExtraClasses"
           TestId="@TestId"
           @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">@Status</Badge>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The status word, exactly as it was authored. Nothing in,
    /// nothing out: a block that states no status should not leave an empty
    /// badge on the line.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Status { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which folder the status was read from. Without it the status is
    /// still shown, just not judged.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public KnowledgeFolder Folder { get; set; } = KnowledgeFolder.Unknown;

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    private bool Unrecognised => KnowledgeStatusBadge.IsUnrecognised(Folder, Status);

    /// <summary>The flag, then the caller's own class. Badge appends this whole
    /// string after the badge classes, and the caller's part goes last within it,
    /// so nothing a caller passes displaces a class the stylesheet or a test
    /// matches on by exact position.</summary>
    private string? ExtraClasses
    {
        get
        {
            var flag = Unrecognised ? "knowledge-status--unrecognised" : string.Empty;

            if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass)) return flag.Length == 0 ? null : flag;

            return flag.Length == 0 ? CssClass : $"{flag} {CssClass}";
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Naming the values that were expected is the whole point of
    /// knowing the vocabulary: a status nobody recognises is nearly always a
    /// typo, and a typo is only useful once it is visible.</summary>
    private string? StatusTitle => Unrecognised
        ? $"Unexpected status. Expected one of: {string.Join(", ", KnowledgeStatus.Values(Folder))}."
        : null;
}
